You are working on an enterprise-grade Flask multi-tenant SaaS POS system.

STRICT RULES:

1. Multi-Tenancy
- Every model MUST include tenant_id unless explicitly global
- All queries MUST filter by tenant_id
- Never expose cross-tenant data

2. Architecture
- Use service layer (no business logic in controllers)
- Controllers = request/response only
- Services = business logic
- Models = data only

3. Folder Rules
- core/ = system-wide logic (tenant, billing, payments, delivery)
- modules/ = tenant features (pos, inventory, storefront)
- admin/ = super admin only

4. Delivery Module
- Must be decoupled from POS
- Use service calls, not direct model coupling
- Must support enable/disable per tenant

5. Payments
- Use gateway abstraction for payments (no provider-specific logic in business code)
- Each tenant has separate credentials

6. Code Quality
- Use type hints
- Use reusable services
- Avoid duplication
- Write scalable, modular code

7. Security
- Validate tenant context in every request
- Protect admin routes strictly

8. Future-Proofing
- Write code that supports:
  - API-first design
  - mobile apps (rider app)
  - third-party integrations

Always refactor code to match these rules.

9. How this repo actually works (do not fight it)
- Much of the web UI still lives in `app/core/flask_app.py` with raw SQL; tenant scoping is enforced via `TenantAwareConnection` / session `tenant_id`, not ORM models everywhere.
- Global tables (no `tenant_id`): e.g. `tenants`, subscription/plan metadata as modeled in migrations — treat reads/writes as cross-tenant only on explicit super-admin or lifecycle paths.
- Super-admin and provisioning code may use `get_raw_connection()` or bypass normal tenant filters; keep that narrow and audited.
- POS checkout uses `sales` / `sale_items`; delivery or storefront may use separate `orders`-style tables — do not assume one unified order model without checking the schema.
- Shared paths and defaults: `app/core/flask_config.py`. Shared web SQL/helpers: `app/modules/web/queries.py`. Server-rendered HTTP handlers: package `app/modules/web/routes/` (`register_web_routes` in `routes/__init__.py`; submodules `auth`, `pos`, `admin`, `inventory`, `owner`, etc.). Registration on the Flask `app` preserves legacy `url_for('login')` endpoint names.
